---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Accessibility • Fynoptic"
  description="How Fynoptic works with keyboards and screen readers, what still needs work, and how to report an accessibility barrier."
  canonical="/accessibility"
  ogTitle="Accessibility • Fynoptic"
  ogDescription="How Fynoptic works with keyboards and screen readers, what still needs work, and how to report an accessibility barrier."
>
  <section class="a11y-page" role="region" aria-labelledby="a11y-title">
    <div class="container a11y-content">
      <h1 id="a11y-title" class="slab-title">Accessibility</h1>

      <p>
        Fynoptic teaches people to spot junk fees, dark patterns, and subscription traps.
        That mission falls apart if the site itself is hard to use, so here's a plain account
        of where things actually stand — what works today, what's still in progress, and how
        to tell us when something doesn't work for you.
      </p>

      <h2>Keyboard use</h2>
      <p>You can operate the whole site without a mouse.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A "Skip to content" link is the first thing focused when you tab in. Activating it
          jumps straight to the main content, past the header navigation.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every dialog — sign in, sign up, password reset, and the other in-page modals across
          the site — traps keyboard focus while it's open, closes on Escape, and returns focus
          to whatever you were doing when it opened.
        </li>
        <li>
          The mobile menu closes with Escape, with its own close button, or by tapping a link
          in it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The fee ticket on the homepage and the feature section further down the page are both
          fully keyboard-operable: arrow keys move between rows or panels, and each one shows
          its own detail as you go.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
      <p>
        If your system is set to reduce motion, Fynoptic follows it. The homepage's rotating
        headline word freezes on the first word instead of cycling. The feature section further
        down the homepage swaps its scroll-driven, blurring effect for a plain, static set of
        tabs — nothing is left blurred or invisible. We test this directly rather than just
        hoping the CSS holds up.
      </p>

      <h2>High contrast and dyslexia-friendly type</h2>
      <p>
        This one is honest work-in-progress. The site can remember a high-contrast preference
        and a dyslexia-friendly typeface preference, but there's no visible switch to turn
        either one on yet — the plumbing exists, the button doesn't. If you need either of
        these today, email us and we'll help directly while we finish building the control.
      </p>

      <h2>Color contrast</h2>
      <p>
        We don't have a published contrast-ratio target yet, and an internal audit has already
        found spots that likely fall short, particularly on text near transitions and
        animations. We haven't fixed all of it. If something is hard to read because of low
        contrast, that's exactly the kind of report that helps us fix the right things first.
      </p>

      <h2>Report a barrier</h2>
      <p>
        If anything on Fynoptic doesn't work with a keyboard, a screen reader, or otherwise
        gets in your way, tell us. Email <a href="mailto:info@fynoptic.org">info@fynoptic.org</a>
        with what page you were on and what happened — we read every message.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
